  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schemas)} slot="head" />

  <!-- HERO & SUMMARY SECTION -->
  <section class="hero-banner" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <h1 id="hero-title" class="hero-banner-title speakable-headline">
      Japan Time Zone Guide: <span class="gradient-text">JST, UTC+9 &amp; Tokyo Time</span>
    </h1>
  </section>

  <div class="editorial-meta-bar" aria-label="Editorial metadata">
    <span>Last Updated: <strong>July 30, 2026</strong></span>
    <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
    <span>Editorially Reviewed by <a href="/author/jackie-bruce/"><strong>Jackie Bruce</strong></a></span>
    <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
    <span>Data Source: <a href="https://www.nict.go.jp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>NICT Japan</strong></a></span>
  </div>

  <section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-label="Quick Guide Summary">
    <div class="info-callout speakable-summary" style="background: var(--panel); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; border-radius: 6px;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Quick Answer: What Time Zone is Japan In?</h3>
      <p>
        Japan operates on a single national time zone called <strong>Japan Standard Time (JST)</strong>, which is fixed at an offset of <strong>UTC+9</strong> (nine hours ahead of Coordinated Universal Time). Japan does <strong>not</strong> observe Daylight Saving Time (DST), meaning its clocks remain unchanged throughout the year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether you are checking the current time in <a href="/tokyo-time/">Tokyo</a>, Osaka, Kyoto, or Sapporo, the entire Japanese nation shares the exact same time. The official meridian for JST is located at 135° East longitude in Akashi City, Hyogo Prefecture.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reading-meta" style="margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
      <span>⏱️ Estimated Reading Time: <strong>21 minutes</strong></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TABLE OF CONTENTS -->
  <section class="table-of-contents reveal" aria-label="Table of Contents" style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 2rem 0;">
    <span class="toc-kicker" style="font-weight: bold; color: var(--accent); text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.85rem;">Detailed Navigation</span>
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">Guide Contents</h2>
    <ol style="line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><a href="#overview-japan-time-zone">1. Overview of Japan Standard Time (JST)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#akashi-meridian-history">2. The Akashi Meridian &amp; History of Time in Japan</a></li>
      <li><a href="#why-no-dst-in-japan">3. Why Japan Does Not Observe Daylight Saving Time</a></li>
      <li><a href="#regional-solar-variations">4. Solar Time Differences (Hokkaido to Okinawa)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#japan-vs-world-conversions">5. Japan Time vs World Time Zones Comparison Table</a></li>
      <li><a href="#business-trading-tokyo">6. Financial Markets &amp; Scheduling with Tokyo</a></li>
      <li><a href="#developer-guide-jst">7. Developer Guide: Handling `Asia/Tokyo` &amp; Atomic Synchronization</a></li>
      <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">8. Frequently Asked Questions (20 FAQs)</a></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- SVG DIAGRAM -->
  <section class="content-section" aria-label="Japan Time Zone Diagram">
    <h2>Japan Standard Time (JST) Geographic Meridian</h2>
    <p>
      The diagram below illustrates the geographic longitude of the 135° E meridian passing through Akashi, Japan, and how JST relates to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) and surrounding Asian-Pacific nations.
    </p>

    <div class="svg-container" style="width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0;">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 800 300" width="100%" height="auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="background: var(--panel); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--line, #333);">
        <text x="400" y="30" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="bold">Japan Standard Time (JST) — Fixed UTC+9 Offset</text>
        
        <!-- Longitude Line Diagram -->
        <rect x="50" y="70" width="700" height="60" fill="#1e293b" rx="6" />
        <line x1="400" y1="60" x2="400" y2="140" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="4" />
        <text x="400" y="160" fill="#f59e0b" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="bold">135° E Meridian (Akashi City, Hyogo)</text>
        <text x="400" y="105" fill="#fff" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="bold">JST = UTC + 9 Hours (Year-Round)</text>
        
        <!-- Geographic Solar Span -->
        <rect x="50" y="190" width="700" height="90" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--line, #444)" rx="6" />
        <text x="70" y="215" fill="currentColor" font-size="13" font-weight="bold">Solar Time Extremes across Japan Archipelago:</text>
        <text x="70" y="240" fill="currentColor" font-size="12">• Easternmost (Nemuro, Hokkaido): 145°35' E — Solar noon occurs ~42 min ahead of JST</text>
        <text x="70" y="260" fill="currentColor" font-size="12">• Westernmost (Yonaguni, Okinawa): 122°56' E — Solar noon occurs ~48 min behind JST</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <section id="overview-japan-time-zone" class="content-section">
    <h2>1. Overview of Japan Standard Time (JST)</h2>
    <p>
      Japan operates under a uniform timekeeping system across its entire territory. Known officially as Japan Standard Time (JST; in Japanese: 日本標準時 <em>Nihon Hyōjunshi</em>), the national clock is permanently fixed at <strong>UTC+9</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because Japan does not adjust for Daylight Saving Time, calculating time differences with countries that observe DST requires noting seasonal switches in partner nations. For instance, when converting between <a href="/tokyo-time/">Tokyo Time</a> and US Eastern Time, the difference shifts between 14 hours during winter (<a href="/gmt-to-est/">EST</a>) and 13 hours during summer (<a href="/est-vs-edt/">EDT</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Learn more about global time zone standards in our <a href="/world-time-zones-guide/">World Time Zones Guide</a> or explore neighboring time zones like <a href="/china-time-zone/">China Time Zone</a> and <a href="/india-time-zone/">India Time Zone</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2 -->
  <section id="akashi-meridian-history" class="content-section">
    <h2>2. The Akashi Meridian &amp; History of Time in Japan</h2>
    <p>
      Standard time in Japan was codified on July 13, 1886, through Imperial Ordinance No. 51. The decree established that the standard time for the entire Japanese empire would be determined by the solar meridian passing through 135° East longitude.
    </p>
    <p>
      This meridian passes directly through <strong>Akashi City</strong> in Hyogo Prefecture, near Kobe. As a result, Akashi has built a rich civic identity around timekeeping and is home to the famous Akashi Planetarium and Meridian Center.
    </p>

    <div class="callout" style="background: var(--panel); border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; padding: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
      <h4>Historical Note: Two Time Zones (1895–1937)</h4>
      <p>
        Following the First Sino-Japanese War, Japan acquired Taiwan and Okinawa, leading to the creation of two time zones in 1895:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Central Standard Time (UTC+9):</strong> Main islands of Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, and Shikoku.</li>
        <li><strong>Western Standard Time (UTC+8):</strong> Taiwan, the Yaeyama and Miyako islands of Okinawa.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In September 1937, Imperial Ordinance No. 529 abolished Western Standard Time, consolidating all territories under Central Standard Time (UTC+9).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="why-no-dst-in-japan" class="content-section">
    <h2>3. Why Japan Does Not Observe Daylight Saving Time</h2>
    <p>
      Following the defeat of Japan in World War II, the Allied Occupation authorities (SCAP) introduced Daylight Saving Time in 1948. Clocks were moved forward 1 hour from May to September.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, the policy met widespread public resistance. Japanese farmers complained that early morning agricultural routines were disrupted, while factory workers reported increased exhaustion because societal work traditions meant staying late regardless of clock position. In 1951, Japan's Diet voted to abolish DST, and the country returned to standard UTC+9 in 1952.
    </p>
    <p>
      Although modern proposals arise occasionally—such as energy-saving proposals following the 2011 Fukushima earthquake or heat mitigation proposals for outdoor marathons—the high cost of updating national infrastructure and strong public preference for consistency have kept Japan on fixed standard time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section id="regional-solar-variations" class="content-section">
    <h2>4. Solar Time Differences (Hokkaido to Okinawa)</h2>
    <p>
      Because Japan spans roughly 22 degrees of longitude, natural solar noon varies across the archipelago:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Eastern extremity (Nemuro, Hokkaido - 145°35' E):</strong> Solar noon occurs 42 minutes before civil 12:00 JST. Sunrise in June occurs as early as 03:35 a.m.</li>
      <li><strong>Central benchmark (Akashi, Hyogo - 135°00' E):</strong> Solar noon matches civil 12:00 JST exactly.</li>
      <li><strong>Western extremity (Yonaguni, Okinawa - 122°56' E):</strong> Solar noon occurs 48 minutes after civil 12:00 JST. Sunset in July extends until 07:35 p.m.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="japan-vs-world-conversions" class="content-section">
    <h2>5. Japan Time vs World Time Zones Comparison Table</h2>
    <p>
      The table below provides a quick reference for converting Japan Standard Time (JST, UTC+9) to major global cities:
    </p>
    <table class="data-table" style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background: var(--panel); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Location</th>
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Standard Time Difference vs JST</th>
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Daylight Saving Difference vs JST</th>
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Tool / Guide</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">New York (US East)</td>
