          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Sydney, Australia</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Sydney is 1 hour ahead</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Sydney is 2 hours ahead</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);"><a href="/australia-time-zones/">Australia Time Zones</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Beijing, China</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Beijing is 1 hour behind</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Beijing is 1 hour behind</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);"><a href="/china-time-zone/">China Time Zone Guide</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Paris / Berlin</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Europe is 8 hours behind</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Europe is 7 hours behind</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);"><a href="/cet-to-est/">CET to EST Converter</a></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="business-trading-tokyo" class="content-section">
    <h2>6. Financial Markets &amp; Scheduling with Tokyo</h2>
    <p>
      The Tokyo financial market is a critical pillar of Asia-Pacific trading. The Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE) operates on the following schedule:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Morning Session:</strong> 09:00 to 11:30 JST</li>
      <li><strong>Lunch Intermission:</strong> 11:30 to 12:30 JST</li>
      <li><strong>Afternoon Session:</strong> 12:30 to 15:30 JST</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When scheduling cross-border business meetings between North America and Japan:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>08:00 JST (Tokyo morning)</strong> corresponds to <strong>19:00 EDT (Previous Evening in New York)</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>17:00 JST (Tokyo evening)</strong> corresponds to <strong>04:00 EDT (Early Morning in New York)</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section id="developer-guide-jst" class="content-section">
    <h2>7. Developer Guide: Handling `Asia/Tokyo` &amp; Atomic Synchronization</h2>
    <p>
      Because Japan does not observe Daylight Saving Time, working with Japanese timestamps in software applications is highly reliable. However, developers should maintain standard precision practices:
    </p>
    <ol style="line-height: 1.8;">
      <li><strong>IANA Database Key:</strong> Always specify <code>Asia/Tokyo</code> in date formatting libraries.</li>
      <li><strong>Radio Clock Synchronization (JJY):</strong> Japan operates low-frequency time signal stations JJY at 40 kHz (Fukushima) and 60 kHz (Saga). Network Time Protocol (NTP) servers in Japan synchronize directly with NICT atomic clocks.</li>
      <li><strong>24-Hour and 30-Hour Formatting:</strong> Japanese media schedules often feature hours past midnight (e.g., 25:00 for 01:00 the following day). Developers parsing Japanese broadcast metadata must handle these custom conventions.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 8: FAQS -->
  <section id="frequently-asked-questions" class="content-section">
    <h2>8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-accordion">
      {meta.faqs.map((faq) => (
        <details class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; padding: 1rem; background: var(--panel); border-radius: 6px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: bold; cursor: pointer; font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--accent);">
            {faq.q}
